From 72f086e407246e96e040d782fe8020d5cc1354f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 09:21:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Remove all Engineer platform access; add a Contractor-only
 claim PDF report

CEIP is now Contractor-only end to end - there is no Engineer login role
and no interactive page or JSON API the Engineer (or anyone without a
CEIP account) can reach.

- User.role no longer defaults to "Engineer"; it defaults to
  "Contractor". New migration b2c4d6e8f0a1 normalizes any existing
  Engineer-role rows.
- The claim access magic link no longer resolves to an interactive page
  or accepts any write. GET /public/claims/{token}/pdf is now the entire
  public surface: it validates the token and streams a read-only PDF
  claim report, nothing else. Removed the JSON overview endpoint and
  both PATCH endpoints (respond to fact, submit engineer response) from
  the backend, and deleted EngineerClaimReviewPage.jsx and the /review
  route from the frontend entirely.
- Added a real claim report PDF (pdf_service.generate_claim_report_pdf):
  claim summary, the Sub-Clause 20.2 deadline clock, the fact-agreement
  register with its day-count summary, linked events, and response
  history - built from a new shared claim_service.get_claim_report_data
  so the authenticated download and the public link always produce the
  same document.
- Added GET /claims/{claim_id}/report/pdf so the Contractor can download
  that same PDF directly from their own session (to print, email, or
  send via Telegram themselves) without generating a share link first.
- ClaimDetailPage's share panel now offers both: a direct PDF download,
  and a "generate link" action that points straight at the public PDF
  endpoint instead of an in-app page.
- Kept the Contractor's own manual entry of the Engineer's response
  (WorkflowActions / FactsRegister / the engineer-response and
  fact-respond endpoints) - that's the Contractor's own record of what
  the Engineer said via email/letter, not the Engineer touching the
  platform, and it's what makes the Sub-Clause 20.2 deadline clock work.

Multi-tenant auth (organizations, cross-tenant scoping) from the
previous patch is intentionally NOT included here - this is a separate,
standalone patch built directly on the original MVP so it can be
tested without that overhead; apply the auth patch separately later,
at deploy time.
